31:2. **Select the level.** Highest row that applies; when unsure between two, take the higher. The level may rise during verification, never fall. Done when the level and a one-line reason are written down.
32-
33:   | Level | Applies when the change | Minimum evidence |
34-   |---|---|---|
35-   | **Strict** | touches authentication or authorization, a security boundary, secrets or crypto, data migration or deletion, money or other critical business logic, an interface with consumers outside the diff (public API, schema, shared library), or concurrency | Standard, plus: probes of the deny, failure, and boundary cases, not only the happy path; every caller of a changed interface found by search and checked; the real artifact exercised (running app, migration up and down on a disposable database, contract test); the report flags the change for human review |
36-   | **Standard** | changes runtime behavior in any other way: bug fixes, features, refactors, ordinary config | Static checks on touched code; existing tests that cover each claim, or probes where none do; for a bug fix, the reported input shown failing at the base revision and passing on the change; the project's full fast suite once at the end; runtime check when a claim is only observable at runtime |
37-   | **Light** | changes no runtime behavior: docs, comments, config nothing reads at runtime | The project's doc or format checks if it has them; runnable examples and links that changed actually work; the diff read to confirm nothing else changed |
--
39:   Diff size never lowers the level. A one-line change to a permission check is Strict.
40-
41-3. **Find the commands.** Read the repo's instructions (`AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, README), its manifests (`package.json` scripts, `Makefile`, `pyproject.toml`, `justfile`, `Cargo.toml`), and its CI workflow. Prefer the commands CI runs. Find the tests that cover changed code by searching for imports and uses of the changed symbols. Done when you have a list of real commands, each with the claim it covers and a rough cost. A command you could not find in the repo is a gap, not a guess.
42-
43-4. **Plan.** Map each claim to the cheapest check that establishes it, in this order: static checks on touched files, existing targeted tests, the full fast suite, probes, runtime checks. A check with no claim to serve is not run. Done when every claim has a check or is listed as a gap.
--
